Lab 17: Creating and Managing Domains in OpenStack
Objectives
Understand OpenStack Keystone domain concepts
Create and configure domains for multi-tenancy
Assign domain administrators and roles
Implement resource isolation between domains
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with admin privileges
Keystone identity service installed
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient)
Basic understanding of OpenStack concepts
Lab Setup
Ensure you have admin credentials loaded:
source admin-openrc.sh
Verify connectivity:
openstack token issue
Task 1: Creating and Configuring Domains
Subtask 1.1: Create New Domains
# Create two new domains
openstack domain create --description "Engineering Department" engineering
openstack domain create --description "Marketing Department" marketing
Expected Output:

+-------------+----------------------------------+
| Field       | Value                            |
+-------------+----------------------------------+
| description | Engineering Department           |
| enabled     | True                             |
| id          | 03e0870dc8a944a1a286a1d704a03d5c |
| name        | engineering                      |
| options     | {}                               |
| tags        | []                               |
+-------------+----------------------------------+
Subtask 1.2: List and Verify Domains
openstack domain list
Troubleshooting Tip: If domains don't appearing, ensure you have admin privileges by checking your admin-openrc.sh file contains proper credentials.

Task 2: Assigning Administrators and Roles
Subtask 2.1: Create Domain-Specific Users
# Create users for each domain
openstack user create --domain engineering --password EngPass123 eng_admin
openstack user create --domain marketing --password MktPass123 mkt_admin
Subtask 2.2: Assign Admin Roles
# Get the admin role ID
ADMIN_ROLE_ID=$(openstack role show admin -c id -f value)

# Assign admin role to domain users
openstack role add --domain engineering --user eng_admin $ADMIN_ROLE_ID
openstack role add --domain marketing --user mkt_admin $ADMIN_ROLE_ID
Verification:

openstack role assignment list --user eng_admin --domain engineering
openstack role assignment list --user mkt_admin --domain marketing
Task 3: Isolating Resources Between Domains
Subtask 3.1: Create Domain-Specific Projects
openstack project create --domain engineering eng_project
openstack project create --domain marketing mkt_project
Subtask 3.2: Verify Resource Isolation
# As engineering admin
unset OS_PROJECT_NAME OS_PROJECT_DOMAIN_NAME
export OS_USERNAME=eng_admin
export OS_PASSWORD=EngPass123
export OS_USER_DOMAIN_NAME=engineering
export OS_PROJECT_NAME=eng_project
export OS_PROJECT_DOMAIN_NAME=engineering

# Try to list projects (should only see eng_project)
openstack project list
Expected Behavior: The engineering admin should only see projects within their domain.

Subtask 3.3: Cross-Domain Access Test
# Attempt to access marketing project (should fail)
openstack project show mkt_project
Expected Error: HTTP 403 Forbidden - demonstrating proper isolation

Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Successfully created and configured multiple domains in OpenStack Keystone
Established domain-specific administrators with appropriate roles
Demonstrated resource isolation between domains
Key Concepts Reinforced:

Domains provide the highest level of organizational isolation in OpenStack
Role assignments can be scoped to specific domains
Multi-tenancy is achieved through domain and project hierarchy
Cleanup (Optional)
# Switch back to admin credentials
source admin-openrc.sh

# Remove resources
openstack user delete eng_admin mkt_admin
openstack project delete eng_project mkt_project
openstack domain delete engineering marketing
